# encoder vs [display]: the [display] circuit outranks a circuit's own screen.
# Spec: hardware.md §6.12 "When more of these events happen at the same time, the
# latter one in the upper list takes precedence": Library > [display] > Circuit >
# Control. Modelled as (a) an ordinary linger block and (b) a tier that decides
# same-tick collisions, so precedence is an ORDER, not a permanent lockout — an
# encoder you turn later must still be able to take the screen back.
#
#   t0: [display] writes 0.2   -> owner = the display circuit,
#                                 linger 0.05 s = 5 ticks -> lingerUntil = 5
#   t1: the knob is turned -> the encoder wants 1425, but 1 < 5 and it is the
#       lower tier -> blocked; the screen stays on 0.2
#   t2..t4: still blocked (the encoder keeps re-attempting, baseline unadvanced)
#   t5: linger expired -> 5 >= 5 -> the encoder is accepted, screen 1425
tickrate 100
patch <<<
[db8e]

[display]
    value  = I1
    linger = 0.05

[encoder]
    encoder      = E1.1
    smooth       = 0
    outputscale  = 2650
    outputoffset = 100
    output       = O1
>>>
@0 set I1 0.2
@0 expectdisplay D1 value 0.2 tol 1e-6
@1 turn E1.1 48
@1 expectdisplay D1 value 0.2 tol 1e-6
@2 expectdisplay D1 value 0.2 tol 1e-6
@4 expectdisplay D1 value 0.2 tol 1e-6
@5 expectdisplay D1 value 1425 tol 1e-3
